model {
  extend basalt.releaseUpdates {
    metadata {
      libraries "node:http"
      endpointHosts "127.0.0.1"
      constraint "Preview model acceptance registers one run-owned model-only native computer through the existing public signed control route. Its real model key remains sealed on the server; each forwarded request carries a short-lived assertion bound to that grant, computer, pool, method and path. The native client receives an ephemeral loopback proxy and random nonce; only the exact approved model and messages route can reach the pool. Every dispatch requires a fresh live pool view with active subscription quota and disabled extra usage for every selectable account of the required provider. Tools remain on the separate preview stage. Shutdown closes the proxy, revokes that exact computer and confirms the exact revoked grant returns unauthorized before inference. Keys remain in memory and failures expose only closed categories. This changes no pool policy, account selection, deployment resource or production tool authority."
    }
  }
  extend basalt.deployment {
    metadata {
      libraries "ws|node:http"
      endpointHosts "127.0.0.1"
      constraint "Preview MCP calls stay on the isolated stage while model inference uses an owned loopback endpoint. Live model provider inference receives only a nonce for the exact approved model. The native client uses a synthetic Responses peer and unsigned fixture identity; the actual staged installer and installed CLI still exercise model transport, MCP, undo and revocation. Existing provider auth bytes, including the refresh token, must remain unchanged after install, turn and undo. Stage tool observations are required without claiming stage model observations, and every owned computer, session and fixture is cleaned even after a failed proof."
    }
  }
}
